From 54e2287cbc2a202f1416d87b81f670d2f1bfef34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:54:10 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] dma-buf: defer percpu_ref_exit() to the map's final
 release

percpu_ref_exit()'s documented contract is that the caller ensures the
ref is "no longer in active use". The unmap worker and the
reserve-failure synchronous fallback in dma_buf_io_drop_map() both called
it immediately after unmap, while still holding ctx->map's publication
kref -- but a request only ever needs a kref, not an active reference,
to sit in the block layer. It can hold that kref across an entire
invalidation and then, on reaching a driver's hardware-submission funnel
(e.g. nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active()), attempt a fresh active_tryget against
its cached map pointer -- on a percpu_ref that has already been
percpu_ref_exit()'d.

This is reachable, not exotic: it is the ordinary "request was queued
behind something slow while a migration happened" case, and it now
applies to both call sites -- the FENCED worker and the SYNC fallback --
since the previous commit made nvme's submission funnel, not import, the
thing that acquires ->active.

It has been benign so far only by implementation accident.
__percpu_ref_exit() sets ref->percpu_count_ptr = __PERCPU_REF_ATOMIC_DEAD
and frees the percpu allocation; percpu_ref_tryget_live_rcu() tests
!(percpu_count_ptr & __PERCPU_REF_DEAD) before it would dereference
ref->data, which exit() has also set to NULL. So the tryget returns false
instead of a NULL-dereference or a use-after-free -- but nothing in
percpu_ref's documented contract promises that ordering; it is not
something to build correctness on.

Move percpu_ref_exit() out of both call sites and into
dma_buf_io_map_free_rcu(), the kref's RCU-delayed free callback. By
construction no kref holder remains once that callback runs, so the "no
longer in active use" contract actually holds there.

The seg_shift driver-bug unwind path in dma_buf_io_create_map() is
unaffected: that map was never published to ctx->map, so no kref holder
other than the caller about to unwind it can exist, and percpu_ref_exit()
right there is the ordinary init-failure use the function's own kdoc
describes.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
index a52386cdc964..c83769e5ed00 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@ static void dma_buf_io_map_free_rcu(struct rcu_head *rcu)
 	struct dma_buf_io_map *map = container_of(rcu, struct dma_buf_io_map, rcu);
 	struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx = map->ctx;
 
+	percpu_ref_exit(&map->active);
 	kfree(map);
 	dma_buf_io_ctx_put(ctx);
 }
@@ -82,7 +83,10 @@ static void dma_buf_io_map_release_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	dma_resv_unlock(dmabuf->resv);
 
 	dma_fence_put(fence);
-	percpu_ref_exit(&map->active);
+	/*
+	 * percpu_ref_exit() deliberately does not happen here; see
+	 * dma_buf_io_map_free_rcu().
+	 */
 	kref_put(&map->refs, __dma_buf_io_map_free);
 }
 
@@ -205,7 +209,12 @@ static void dma_buf_io_drop_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx)
 		ctx->dev_ops->unmap(ctx, map);
 		kfree(map->fence);
 		map->fence = NULL;
-		percpu_ref_exit(&map->active);
+		/*
+		 * percpu_ref_exit() deliberately does not happen here; see
+		 * dma_buf_io_map_free_rcu(). A request holding only a kref
+		 * can still attempt a fresh active_tryget against this exact
+		 * map after wait_for_completion() above returns.
+		 */
 		kref_put(&map->refs, __dma_buf_io_map_free);
 		return;
 	}
